an ticket in his home state"], "rationale": "With no New Jersey polls, expert ratings, market prices, or fundraising figures available, the forecast rests on the state's four-point Democratic partisan lean in an open-seat contest; national 2028 polling shows competitive hypotheticals but nothing state-specific, so the margin stays near that lean with wide uncertainty.", "watch": "Early general-election polling, expert ratings, and the shape of both parties' tickets — including whether Chris Christie runs — could move New Jersey several points in either direction.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "New Jersey's presidential race is an open contest: the incumbent president is term-limited and neither party has a settled nominee. The state's partisan lean favors Democrats by about four points, and that fundamental is the main anchor for the forecast. No general-election polls of New Jersey, expert ratings, or market prices are available yet, and no fundraising figures have been reported.\n\nNational polling on hypothetical 2028 matchups shows competitive races, and a poll reported by New Jersey media found a Democrat defeating Vance and Rubio, but nothing measures the state's general electorate directly. With only fundamentals to go on, New Jersey rates as a modest Democratic lean with wide uncertainty. Early developments, including Chris Christie weighing a third presidential bid, could reshape both tickets before a general-election picture forms.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6761, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.642, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6472, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6427, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6518, "margin":